From 324034d917c964e034f8c321bb0e23d0b9a5aef8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Turnstone <turnstone@agents.invalid>
Date: Sun, 11 Oct 2026 16:58:20 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Turnstone: a hungry bot eats

The operator asked for auto-eat by name on 2026-10-11, which is the explicit
approval the gate requires; the roadmap's P1-04 had said automatic eating was
not implied by its own approval, so the divergence is recorded in
docs/planning/2026-10-11/implementation-turnstone-autoeat.md rather than left
implicit. Nothing here renames anything: the rebrand half of the gate is
untouched.

New EatBehavior (a behaviour, not a process - eating wants the hand for a
second and a half, not the path, the goal or the task, and every ownership
handover is a way to lose a path, so this needs none of P0-03) and a
Minecraft-free EatPolicy with its own unit tests.

What it is allowed to do is narrowed rather than widened: ordinary food only
(no golden apple, no chorus fruit, and nothing that applies an effect - which
is what rotten flesh, spider eye, pufferfish and poisonous potato have in
common), it never changes what is in the hand while a process is in control,
it waits for the hand to actually arrive before pressing, it takes food from
the main inventory only when the user has allowed Baritone to move items, and
it gives up after three attempts per hunger episode instead of clicking at
nothing. A meal that lands resets the budget, because a second helping for a
bar that is still short is a new ask and not a retry.

One press is not a bite. Vanilla calls releaseUsingItem while the real use key
is up, so a lone processRightClick starts a mouthful the game drops on the next
tick - an instrumented run shows phase=EATING with using=false. The click
helpers are the wrong shape for a fix (re-clicking a mouthful starts it over),
so the button is held through the input layer instead, as a one-tick lease:
ask again to keep holding. It is asked for rather than switched because
clearAllKeys() deliberately does not lift it - every movement calls that on
every tick of a walk, and a meal eaten on the way somewhere must not come off
the mouth twenty times a second.

Both census tests caught this, as designed. ButtonWritesTest gains an entry
(and the reason: a held item use cannot go through BlockPlaceHelper, whose
condition is a block under the crosshair) plus shape pins for the wait;
KeyWritesTest needed no new file, because the write went behind
InputOverrideHandler, which is the remedy its own failure message names.

391 tests, 0 failures (383 before). In game, read back from the game itself:
bar 0 -> 5 -> 10 idle with bread 5 -> 4 -> 3; walking a goto of 130 blocks with
bread 5 -> 0; and with the drain stopped, 0 -> 15 exactly at autoEatThreshold
with bread 5 -> 2.
